readings.c1s1:
{{smaller block|With lokkes crulle [curled] as ''they'' were leyd in presse.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Gen. Prol. to C. T. (ed. Morris), l. 81.}}}}

{{smaller block|''Thei'' dide his comaundement, and com to-geder, ''thei'' thre and two squyres only. ''Merlin'' (E. E. T. S.), iii. 645.}}

{{smaller block|''They'' of Italy salute you. Heb. xiii. 24.}}

{{smaller block|These are ''they'' which came out of great tribulation.
{{right|Rev. vii. 14.}}}}

(''b'') Poss. ''their.'' Of or belonging to them: now always preceding the noun, with the value of an attributive adjective.

{{smaller block|Pantasilia come pertly with hir pure maidnes, . . .
(All ''thaire'' colouris by corse were of cleane white).
{{right|''Destruction of Troy'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 10970.}}}}

{{smaller block|Some glory in ''their'' birth, some in ''their'' skill,
Some in ''their'' wealth, some in ''their'' bodies' force.
{{right|''Shak.'', Sonnets, xci.}}}}

{{smaller block|As if God were so beholden to us for our good deeds as to be bound for ''their'' sakes to forgive us our ill ones!
{{right|''Bp. Atterbury'', Sermons, I. ii.}}}}

Sometimes formerly used alone, with the value now given to ''theirs.''

{{smaller block|My clothing keeps me full as warm as ''their'',
My meates unto my taste as pleasing are.
{{right|''Wither'', Motto, C 3 b, repr. (''Nares.'')}}}}

(''c'') Poss. ''theirs.'' That which belongs to them: always used without the noun, and having the value of a nominative or an objective.

{{smaller block|Belfagor and Belyal and Belssabub als
Heyred hem as hyȝly as heuen wer ''thayres.''
{{right|''Alliterative Poems'' (ed. Morris), ii. 1527.}}}}

{{smaller block|This love of ''theirs'' myself have often seen.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. G. of V., iii. 1. 24.}}}}

{{smaller block|Nothing but the name of zeal appears
'Twixt our best actions and the worst of ''theirs.''
{{right|''Sir J. Denham'', Cooper's Hill.}}}}

(''d'') Obj. (acc.), ''them.''

readings.c1s2:
{{smaller block|Bot — if we may with any gyn<br>Mak ''tham'' to do dedly syn;<br>Than with ''tham'' wil I wun and wake.
{{right|''Holy Rood'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 96.}}}}

{{smaller block|For euery off ''thaim'' was full wyse and sage.
{{right|''Rom. of Partenay'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 1624.}}}}

{{smaller block|Let him and ''them'' agree it; they are able to answer for themselves. {{right|''Jer. Taylor'', Works (ed. 1835), II. 235.}}}}

(''e'') Obj. (dat.), ''them.''

{{smaller block|Give ''them'' wine to drink. {{right|Jer. xxxv. 2.}}}}

(''f'') Used for ''those.'' [Now provincial, Eng. and U. S.]

{{smaller block|As if between ''them'' twain there were no strife.
{{right|''Shak.'', Lucrece, l. 405.}}}}

{{smaller block|Let ''they'' ministers preach till they 'm black in the face.
{{right|''Kingsley'', Westward Ho, xxx.}}}}

{{smaller block|Like ''them'' big hotels<br>Where they shift plates, an' let ye live on smells.
{{right|''Lowell'', Biglow Papers, 2d ser., ii.}}}}

'''They say''', it is said: ''they'' meaning persons generally.

{{smaller block|We must not run, ''they say'', into sudden extreams.
{{right|''Milton'', Reformation in Eng., ii.}}}}

{{smaller block|''They say'' he will come far ben, that lad; wha kens but he may come to be Sub-Prior himself?
{{right|''Scott'', Monastery, xiii.}}}}

'''they<sup>2</sup>†''', ''conj.'' and ''adv.'' A Middle English variant of ''though.''

