'''thibet, Thibetan,''' etc. See ''tibet,'' etc.

'''thible''' (thib′l), ''n.'' [Also ''thibel, thivel, theevil, theivil, theedle''; dial. variants of ''dibble''<sup>1</sup>.] 1. A dibble. ''Halliwell.'' [Prov. Eng.]—2. A stick used for stirring broth, porridge, etc.; a pot-stick. [Prov. Eng. or Scotch.]

{{smaller block|The ''thible'' ran round, and the . . . handfuls of meal fell into the water. ''E. Brontë,'' Wuthering Heights, xiii.}}

3†. A slice; a skimmer; a spatula. ''Imp. Dict.''

'''thick''' (thik), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< ME. ''thicke, thikke, thykke,'' rarely ''thig,'' < AS. ''thicce'' = OS. OFries. ''thikki'' = MD. ''dicke,'' D. ''dik'' = MLG. ''dick'' = OHG. ''dicchi,'' MHG. ''dik, dicke,'' G. ''dick'' = Icel. ''thykkr'' (older forms ''thjokkr'' or ''thjökkr'') = Sw. ''tjok'' = Dan. ''tyk'' (Goth. not recorded); cf. OIr. ''tiug'' (< *''tigu''), thick. Cf. ''tight''<sup>1</sup>.] I. ''a.'' 1. Having relatively great extent or depth from one surface to its opposite; being relatively of great depth, or extent from side to side: opposed to ''thin.''

{{smaller block|Thre hundred elne was it [the ark] long,
Naild and sperd, ''thig'' and strong.
{{right|''Genesis and Exodus'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 564.}}}}

{{smaller block|Thou art waxen fat; thou art grown ''thick.''
{{right|Deut. xxxii. 15.}}}}

{{smaller block|If the Sun is incommodious, we have ''thick'' folding Shutters on the out-Side, and thin ones within, to prevent that. ''N. Bailey,'' tr. of Colloquies of Erasmus, I. 198.}}

2. Having (a specified) measurement in a direction perpendicular to that of the length and breadth; measuring (so much) between opposite surfaces: as, a board one inch ''thick.''

{{smaller block|The walles of the gallery are about two yardes ''thicke'' at the least. ''Coryat,'' Crudities, I. 33.}}

readings.c2s3:
{{smaller block|Of Fruits, he reckons the Iacapucaya, like a pot, as big as a great bowle, two fingers ''thicke'', with a couer on it, within full of Chesnuts. {{right|''Purchas'', Pilgrimage, p. 843.}}}}

3. Having numerous separate parts or individuals set or occurring close together; dense; compactly arranged.

{{smaller block|He is the pyes patroun and putteth it in hire ere,
That there the thorne is ''thikkest'' to buylden and brede.
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (B), xii. 228.}}}}

{{smaller block|We supposed him some French mans sonne, because he had a ''thicke'' blacke bush beard, and the Salvages seldome haue any at all.
{{right|Quoted in ''Capt. John Smith's'' Works, I. 184.}}}}

{{smaller block|We caught another snow-storm, so ''thick'' and blinding that we dared not venture out of the harbor.
{{right|''B. Taylor'', Northern Travel, p. 16.}}}}

4. Having relatively great consistency; also, containing much solid matter in suspension or solution; approaching the consistency of a solid; inspissated: as, ''thick'' cream; ''thick'' paste; often of liquids, turbid; muddy; cloudy.

{{smaller block|I can selle
Bothe dregges and draffe, and drawe it at on hole,
''Thikke'' ale and thinne ale. ''Piers Plowman'' (B), xix. 398.}}

{{smaller block|Forth gusht a stream of gore blood ''thick.''
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., II. i. 39.}}}}

{{smaller block|Make the gruel ''thick'' and slab.
{{right|''Shak.'', Macbeth, iv. 1. 32.}}}}

{{smaller block|At the end, or snout, of the glacier this water issues forth, not indeed as a clear bright spring, but as a ''thick'' stream laden with detritus. ''Huxley'', Physiography, p. 161.}}

5. Heavy; profound; intense; extreme; great.

{{smaller block|Moyses sithen held up is hond,
And ''thikke'' therknesse cam on that lond.
{{right|''Genesis and Exodus'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 3102.}}}}

readings.c3s1:
{{smaller block|Bote euer-more Seraphe askes and cries,
"Where was Eualac?" the stour was so ''thikke''.
{{right|''Joseph of Arimathie'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 18.}}}}

{{smaller block|''Thick'' slumber
Hangs upon mine eyes.
{{right|''Shak.'', Pericles, v. 1. 235.}}}}

6. Obscure; not clear; especially, laden with clouds or vapor; misty; foggy: noting the atmosphere, the weather, etc.

{{smaller block|It continued ''thick'' and boisterous all the night.
{{right|''Winthrop'', Hist. New England, I. 22.}}}}

{{smaller block|Again the evening closes, in ''thick'' and sultry air;
There 's thunder on the mountains, the storm is gathering there.
{{right|''Bryant'', Count of Greiers.}}}}

7. Mentally dull; stupid; devoid of intelligence: as, to have a ''thick'' head.

{{smaller block|He a good wit? hang him, baboon! his wit 's as ''thick'' as Tewksbury mustard.
{{right|''Shak.'', 2 Hen. IV., ii. 4. 262.}}}}

{{smaller block|What if you think our reasons ''thick'', and our ground of separation mistaken?
{{right|''Penn'', Liberty of Conscience, v.}}}}

8. Mentally clouded; befogged; slow, weak, or defective in sense-perception, sometimes in moral perception: as, to be ''thick'' of sight, hearing, etc.: said of persons or of the organs of sense.

{{smaller block|The people muddied,
''Thick'' and unwholesome in their thoughts and whispers.
{{right|''Shak.'', Hamlet, iv. 5. 82.}}}}

{{smaller block|My sight was ever ''thick'';
. . . tell me what thou notest about the field.
{{right|''Shak.'', J. C., v. 3. 21.}}}}

{{smaller block|I am ''thick'' of hearing,
Still, when the wind blows southerly.
{{right|''Ford'', Broken Heart, ii. 1.}}}}

{{smaller block|A cloudlike change,
In passing, with a grosser film made ''thick''
These heavy, horny eyes.}}

readings.c3s2:
{{smaller block|{{right|''Tennyson'', St. Simeon Stylites.}}}}

9. Indistinct in utterance; inarticulate; not clear.

{{smaller block|He rose and walked up and down the room, and finally spoke in a ''thick'', husky voice, as one who pants with emotion.
{{right|''H. B. Stowe'', Oldtown, p. 460.}}}}

10. Abounding; filled; plentifully supplied: followed by ''with'' (formerly ''of'' or ''for'').

{{smaller block|The Westerne shore by which we sayled we found all along well watered, but very mountanous and barren, the vallies very fertill, but extreame ''thicke of'' small wood so well as trees.
{{right|Quoted in ''Capt. John Smith's'' Works, I. 176.}}}}

{{smaller block|His reign [Henry III.'s] was not onely long for continuance, fifty-six years, but also ''thick for'' remarkable mutations happening therein.
{{right|''Fuller'', Ch. Hist., III. iv. 24.}}}}

{{smaller block|The air was ''thick with'' falling snow.
{{right|''Bryant'', Two Travellers.}}}}

{{smaller block|She looked up at Eve, her eyes ''thick with'' tears.
{{right|''Harper's Mag.'', LXXVIII. 449.}}}}

11. Numerous; plentiful; frequent; crowded.

{{smaller block|Thei were so ''thikke'' and so entacched ech amonge other, that mo than a thousand fill in to the river.
{{right|''Merlin'' (E. E. T. S.), ii. 288.}}}}

{{smaller block|These [Oxen and Kine] were . . . exceeding ''thicke'' from the one end of the Market place . . . to the other.
{{right|''Coryat'', Crudities, I. 55.}}}}

{{smaller block|The brass-hoof'd steeds tumultuous plunge and bound,
And the ''thick'' thunder beats the lab'ring ground.
{{right|''Pope'', Iliad, xi. 198.}}}}

{{smaller block|Lay me,
When I shall die, within some narrow grave,
Not by itself—for that would be too proud—
But where such graves are ''thickest''.
{{right|''Browning'', Paracelsus.}}}}

12. Being of a specified number; numbering. [Rare.]

readings.c3s3:
¶
{{smaller block|There is a guard of spies ten ''thick'' upon her.
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Volpone, i. 1.}}}}

13. Close in friendship; intimate. [Colloq.]

{{smaller block|He
Could conjure, tell fortunes, and calculate tides, . . .
And was thought to be ''thick'' with the Man in the Moon.
{{right|''Barham'', Ingoldsby Legends, I. 270.}}}}

{{smaller block|Don't you be getting too ''thick'' with him — he 's got his father's blood in him too.
{{right|''George Eliot'', Mill on the Floss, ii. 6.}}}}

{{smaller|'''Half-thick file.''' See ''file''<sup>1</sup>.— '''Thick coal,''' a bed of coal in the Dudley district, England, averaging about thirty feet in thickness, "a source of enormous wealth to the district" (''Hull'').— '''Thick focaloid, homeoid, intestine.''' See the nouns.— '''Thick limestone.''' Same as ''scar-limestone.''— '''Thick register.''' See ''register''<sup>1</sup>, 5 (''b'').— '''Thick squall.''' See ''squall''<sup>1</sup>.— '''Thick stuff,''' in ''ship-building,'' a general name for all planking above 4 inches in thickness.}}

{{smaller block|All the timber, ''thick-stuff,'' and plank to be fresh-cut.
{{right|''Laslett'', Timber, p. 76.}}}}

{{smaller|'''Thick 'un,''' a sovereign; also, a crown, or five shillings. Sometimes written ''thickun.'' [Cant.]}}

{{smaller block|If you like . . . I will send a few ''thickuns'' to bring you . . . to Start.
{{right|''Cornhill Mag.'', VI. 648.}}}}

{{smaller block|If he feel that it were better for him to quaff the flowing bowl, and he has a drought within him, and a friend or a ''thick 'un'' to stand by him, he is a poor weak cross-grained fool to refuse.
{{right|''Percy Clarke'', The New Chum in Australia, p. 143.}}}}

{{smaller|'''Through thick and thin,''' over smooth or rough places; with or without obstruction; despite all opposition; unwaveringly; steadily.}}
